.TH USHER 1 "2026" "usher 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
usher \- record the Wayland window layout and place windows back
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B usher
.I save | restore | doctor | status | toggle | cleanly | ...
.br
.B usher-mgr
.I watch | resume | display-changed
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B usher
seats windows into a steady arrangement on a wlroots/Wayfire session. Instead of
hand-written placement rules it records where windows actually are and puts them
back. Two commands split the job:
.B usher-mgr
is the service the session starts, and
.B usher
is how you ask anything of it. Placement is PLACE for a
window after login (or a re-arm), then SETTLED: a reopened window just
appears where you are; the opt-in
.I usher/include
anchors keep snapping back even then, and
.I usher/exclude
lists windows never to place.
.PP
An optional
.B usher-indicator
tray icon shows the placement mode and toggles it on a left-click (the
.I indicator
sub-package).
.SH VERBS
.TP
.B watch
The daemon (started at login): record continuously, place on map. Re-running it
reloads a running instance.
.TP
.B save | capture
Snapshot the current layout into the store now, and SAY what it declined to
remember and why: an entry nothing could ever match again is not memory, so
the rule is to log one and never write it. The daemon logs the same refusals
to
.IR watch.log ,
once per window rather than once per pass.
.IP
HANDED TO THE DAEMON WHEN ONE IS RUNNING, because a one-shot verb cannot see
another account's windows individually: the per-window map is filled by a
trigger and lives in the process that was triggered, so a capture from a
shell would record every one of that account's windows under ONE shared key.
The daemon holds the map and owns the store, so it does the capture, which
also makes this the forced capture a store repair needs (capture is otherwise
event-driven, so editing the store and waiting relearns nothing).
.IP
With no daemon and another account's windows present it REFUSES rather than
writing what it cannot see (exit 2, naming the source and the remedy).
Declining costs one command; proceeding writes a collapsed key over geometry
there may be nothing left to recover it from.
.TP
.B restore " [--dry-run] [--only S] [--from SPEC]"
Place the stored layout onto the live session. Without
.B --from
this is the learned knowledge base for the current display profile.
.B --from
restores a stored SNAPSHOT instead:
.I latest
is the newest rolled snapshot (undo a shuffle you just made), while
.IR today ,
.I yesterday
or a
.I YYYY-MM-DD
is that day's FIRST snapshot, the stable "how it looked that morning".
.B --from list
shows what is available.
.TP
.B lock " [--force]"
Lock the seated login session and VERIFY it took, by reading logind's
.I LockedHint
back: `loginctl lock-session` exits 0 whether or not anything is listening, so
its status proves nothing. Non-zero if the session did not actually lock.
usher does not lock anything itself; it asks logind, and whatever the session
registered as its locker does the work.
.TP
.B predict " [--out FILE]"
Record what the NEXT restore should produce: every live window with the
workspace, position and size its remembered slot would give it. Writes
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/usher/prediction.json
unless told otherwise. Always exits 0; this records, it does not judge.
.TP
.B verify " [--against FILE]"
Diff the live layout against a prediction and exit non-zero on any window
that is missing or in the wrong place. This is the only end-to-end check that
is not circular:
.B restore --dry-run
compares the store against the screen, so when something teaches the store a
wrong answer the two agree and it reports everything fine. A prediction
written BEFORE the event cannot have been corrupted by it. Record one, reboot,
then verify.
.IP
A window is paired with its row by app-id AND identity, which is the store's
own key shape. Where several windows share one key the report says so, and the
row counts as
.B ok
if ANY of them came back at the remembered place: a shared key holds exactly
one slot and
.B doctor
will name the collision.
.TP
.B resume
Start/reload in ADOPT mode (adopt the current layout as baseline; do not
restore).
.TP
.B status
Everything the daemon publishes: its mode, the seconds until it settles, the
ring fraction the tray draws, whether it is
.B recording
(learning the layout, which is false only while a display change is in
flux), and how long ago the last transpose was. One line per fact, so every
published field has a reader; a check asserts the two sides of that file
agree.
.TP
.B plugins
List the loaded window plugins (built-in + user).
.TP
.B launch
Respawn saved-but-absent windows now, without starting the daemon.
.TP
.B forget " SELECTOR... [--apply]"
Drop remembered slots. A SELECTOR is a glob over the identity a window is
remembered by, which is what
.B doctor
and
.B restore --dry-run
print:
.I "forget 'chrome:win:*'"
or
.IR "forget 'kitty:/src/*'" .
An entry keyed by its app-id alone has no identity, so there the glob matches
the app-id, which is what
.I "forget signal"
means. PREVIEWS by default and needs
.B --apply
to act, since this deletes learned data; the store is copied aside first
either way. With the daemon running the edit is handed to IT rather than made
here, because it owns the store while it runs: editing the file underneath it
would lose the change to the next capture, which rewrites the whole store from
memory.
.TP
.B reload
Pick up new code in a running daemon and change NOTHING else: no restore, no
re-baselining of the remembered layout, no relaunch. This is what a deploy
wants;
.B resume
looks right for it and is not, because it captures.
.TP
.B wind-down
Bring the session to a clean stop, for a power menu to call before it reboots:
capture while the layout is still true, stop the watcher so nothing overwrites
that capture, then ask each app to exit cleanly under a bounded deadline. Does
not reboot or log out. Always exits 0.
.TP
.B cleanly " login | logoff | reboot | shutdown [--force]"
Wind the session down and then take the action, so a reboot cannot lose
the layout. REFUSES when it cannot reach the compositor while a session is
running, since winding down without a capture would lose the thing this
exists to keep;
.B --force
overrides and says what is being given up.
.B login
starts the console session from a remote shell, through the provider at
.IR ~/.config/usher/session-start .
.IP
THE EXIT CONTRACT, which is what a caller must match.
.B reboot
and
.B shutdown
.I exec
systemctl and so never return on success, while
.B logoff
signals the session and returns; a caller should not have to know which.
A NON-ZERO EXIT MEANS usher DID NOT HAND OVER, so the caller should take the
action itself. Zero means the end state is reached or under way, so it should
not. One idiom is therefore correct for all three:
.IP
.EX
usher cleanly <verb> || <the caller's own fallback>
.EE
.IP
On
.B reboot
and
.B shutdown
the exec makes the right-hand side unreachable; on
.B logoff
the zero return does. A non-zero exit is a refusal, or a
.B logoff
that found no process to signal, and in both the caller's fallback is wanted.
Note the rule holds in ONE direction only: zero does not prove a handover
happened, because a
.B logoff
with no session, and any
.BR --dry-run ,
also exit 0 with nothing to do. That is the direction a caller needs, and it
is the safe one. An unconditional
.B ;
is a deliberate choice rather than a mistake: the fallback then runs on
success too, which for a crude session-end backstop costs nothing, and it
ends the session even when usher is WRONG rather than only when usher reports
that it did not hand over.
It is wrong in exactly one cell, though, and that is
.BR --dry-run :
.B usher cleanly reboot -n ; systemctl reboot
reboots for real. A dry run cannot protect itself here, because no exit code
is safe under both separators (zero defeats
.BR ; ,
non-zero would make
.B ||
do the very thing the dry run is declining to do), which is the sharpest
argument for the idiom above.
.TP
.B display-changed
For a display-change hook. Re-places against the profile for the new monitor
set, and does nothing at all when the set has not actually changed.
.TP
.B place | settle | toggle
Place every window that maps for a while, settle now so new
windows are left alone, or flip between the two. These
govern what happens to a window as it MAPS; they say nothing about windows
already open.
.TP
.B transpose
Sweep the windows that are ALREADY OPEN onto where they belong now. This is
what a display change triggers by itself, and the verb asks for another pass
by hand when one did not quite land.
.IP
It is a BLIP: it issues placements and returns, holding nothing and waiting
for nothing, so there is no mode to leave and nothing to turn off afterwards.
Every open window is re-graced and owed one placement, since a window that
mapped hours ago is otherwise past its grace and would be refused. The
read-backs that follow are the ordinary placement verification, not part of
this.
.IP
Distinct from
.B place
on purpose: that decides what happens to NEW windows and stays in force,
where this acts once on the windows there are. Exit 2 with no daemon running,
since placement is the service's job and nothing was assessed.
.TP
.B stop
Stop the running daemon cleanly.
.TP
.B exclude | include
Show the never-place and anchor rules as loaded, with any parse errors.
.TP
.B doctor
Report what usher is and is not doing: the store (entries per app, the display
profile, schema), the plugin registry, the cross-tool contracts, and per window
what would be relaunched or placed and WHY not.
.IP
A window usher will not REMEMBER is labelled with the reason, and the four are
not interchangeable:
.I unmatched
is identified fine with nothing stored yet, which the next capture fixes;
.I pending
means ask again shortly;
.I unjoinable
means no join is possible ever (another account's window, or a plugin saying
so); and
.I parked
means its monitor is away, so where it currently sits is not where it belongs
and usher is deliberately not learning it until the display is back. The
reason comes from the plugin that knows.
.IP
Exit 1 on a contract breach;
exit 2 when it could not see the live session at all (no
.BR WAYFIRE_SOCKET ,
no pywayfire, or no compositor answering), in which case the window sections
are SKIPPED rather than answered from nothing and the report says so loudly.
A non-interactive
.BR ssh (1)
carries none of the session environment, which is the usual way to meet it.
.TP
.B selftest
Offline unit checks (no compositor).
.SH SUPPORTING AN APP
Nothing, for the part most people want. Three tiers, and each is only worth
reaching for when the one below falls short.
.TP
.B nothing
gets the window back WHERE AND HOW BIG it was. Any window with a title is
captured, keyed and placed, and a single-window app is keyed by its app-id
ALONE, so the slot does not move when the document does. What you give up is
only the starting.
.TP
.B a desktop entry
gets the app STARTED FOR YOU at login. Write one when the app ships none, when
its entry is
.I Terminal=true
(Debian's
.I vim.desktop
is
.IR "Exec=vim %F, Terminal=true" ,
so it describes terminal vim and usher correctly refuses it for a gvim
window), or when its app-id differs from the entry's filename.
.I StartupWMClass
is then the load-bearing line, since the join is on the app-id.
.TP
.B a plugin
is for when IDENTITY is the problem rather than launching. Any one of these
means the tiers above cannot serve you:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
SEVERAL WINDOWS of one app must be told apart. Without a plugin, two or more
windows of the same app fall back to per-TITLE keys, so the slot follows the
document rather than the window. One window is fine; several is what needs a
plugin, and it is the whole reason the chrome plugin exists.
.IP \(bu 2
THE IDENTITY IS NOT IN THE TITLE. chrome reads a SessionID from the browser's
session file, kitty the shell's cwd from
.IR /proc ,
mux the command the window runs. A title that has to be right at the instant
you look is a bootstrap, not an identity.
.IP \(bu 2
THE RELAUNCH DIFFERS PER WINDOW. A desktop entry describes an APP, so the
default starts one invocation per app; a terminal holding a specific remote
session needs its own command.
.IP \(bu 2
IDENTITY IS NOT READY WHEN THE WINDOW MAPS. A plugin can answer
.RI ( "pending" ", " why )
and usher will neither place nor remember the window until it is sure. Nothing
else can express that, and guessing corrupts the slot it was about to aim at.
.IP \(bu 2
THE APP NEEDS LETTING GO CLEANLY.
.I wind_down
is how chrome gets
.I SessionEnded
instead of
.I Crashed
on a reboot.
.RE
.PP
If none of those is true, a desktop entry is the simpler and more portable
answer: no Python, it benefits your launcher and menus too, and it cannot
break when usher's internals move.

.SS The default relaunch
A window whose app no plugin claims is still relaunched at session start, from
the freedesktop registry: the app-id is joined to a
.I .desktop
entry by filename and then by
.IR StartupWMClass ,
its
.I Exec=
is taken, and the field codes are stripped so the app opens with no document.
.B The only command usher ever runs is an Exec= out of a desktop file it read,
never a stored string and never a
.I /proc
cmdline, which cannot be replayed safely: a browser rewrites its own argv, a
terminal's cmdline carries flags usher itself injected, and a one-off file
argument would reopen a file that is gone.
.PP
It refuses rather than guesses when several entries match, when none does, and
when the registry marks the entry
.IR NoDisplay ", " Hidden ", " Terminal=true
or not an
.IR Application ,
which is what stops a tray applet or a D-Bus portal service being started as
though it were an app. A plugin claiming the app overrides it, and
.I USHER_NO_DEFAULT_RELAUNCH=1
disables it. See
.B doctor
for the command it would run per saved window.
.PP
To have usher relaunch something it cannot work out, WRITE A DESKTOP ENTRY in
.IR ~/.local/share/applications .
An app started from a shell with no entry of its own, or whose entry is
.I Terminal=true
(Debian's
.I vim.desktop
is), is still remembered and still placed when you open it; it is simply not
started for you.
.I StartupWMClass
is the key part, since the join is on the app-id.
.I NoDisplay=true
keeps such an entry out of the application menu and usher still honours it:
NoDisplay means "do not show this in menus" and says nothing about launching,
so it is read as a refusal only for an entry you did NOT author.
.I Hidden=true
is a deletion in the spec's own words and is honoured everywhere.
.SH ANOTHER ACCOUNT'S WINDOWS
A window on this desk may belong to a different system account: a sandboxed
enclave running its own browser inside the session user's compositor. usher
treats those differently in four places, and the division is a privilege
boundary rather than a preference.
.TP
.B what usher does for them
It PLACES them. The compositor's IPC needs no cooperation from the client, so
a remembered slot works whoever owns the window. It keys them by SOURCE, so
another account's app can never change the key shape of this user's copy of
the same app.
.TP
.B what usher refuses
It never LEARNS a window it cannot identify, and it cannot identify one whose
application state lives in a home it may not read. It never starts another
account's app as this user, which would open a second, personal copy of a
remembered work application. And it redacts the title, which is that
account's content.
.TP
.B what it asks for
Starting and recording are that account's own business, because the privilege
is. usher asks through
.I ~/.config/usher/source-trigger
(see FILES) and the account then runs, as itself, one of two verbs that take
no argument ever:
.TP
.B usher-mgr _source-capture
Record which apps THIS account has open, to
.IR roster.json .
Reads them from the compositor over
.IR ext_foreign_toplevel_list_v1 ,
which the compositor filters to this account's own windows, so the filtering
is the scoping and no caller can widen it.
.IP
A ROSTER AND NOT A LAYOUT, and the reason is Wayland's rather than any
policy: no Wayland protocol exposes a window's position, not even to the
window's own client, so the apps this account launched do not know where they
are either. The compositor does know, and is reachable only over an IPC
socket in the session user's 0700 runtime directory. So this side can neither
read a position nor act on one, and placement stays the session user's not as
a restriction but as the only split there is.
.IP
An observation with no windows at all does NOT overwrite a remembered roster,
and says so. Capture while the session is intact, before anything is torn
down; an account captured after its apps are killed would otherwise record an
empty desk, and the next login would restore nothing from a record that looks
perfectly valid.
.TP
.B usher-mgr _source-resume
Start this account's remembered apps that are not running, resolving each
app-id against THIS account's own desktop registry. Refuses rather than
starting blind when it cannot see what is already up, since starting a second
copy of everything is worse than starting nothing.
.IP
Both verbs are internal: they are hidden from the usage text and refused by
the
.B usher
entry point, which is what lets a sudoers grant name a fixed command with no
wildcard. A verb a human types is one that may later grow a flag, and a grant
must not be able to mean something nobody reviewed.
.SH DISPLAY PROFILES
A placement only means anything on the monitors it was learned on, so the
knowledge base is keyed by the CONNECTED DISPLAY SET: docked and undocked
remember separate layouts rather than overwriting one another, and a window
whose remembered output is not attached is reported
.B UNPLACEABLE
rather than skipped in silence.
.PP
.BR hwdp (1)
supplies the profile id when it is installed (a soft dependency). Without it
the id is derived from the outputs the last snapshot saw, and failing that
everything shares one
.I default
profile, which is the historical behaviour. Override with
.BR USHER_PROFILE .
A pre-profile store is adopted into the current profile once, so no learned
placement is lost on upgrade.
.SS Surfaces: a starting point at a desk you have never used
The first dock at a new desk has nothing learned, so every window stays where
it opens. A SURFACE MAP names a part of a panel at that desk, and a name is
the only thing that can carry a window's home across desks: a workspace cell
cannot, since (2,2) does not exist in a 2x2 grid, and a rect cannot, since
1920x1080 means nothing on a portrait panel.
.PP
.BR hwdp (1)
owns the map and resolves it, because turning "the bottom half of that
monitor" into a rect needs the logical size after rotation and scale.
.B usher
reads
.B hwdp surfaces
and records, on each learned entry, which surface it sits in; a window with no
slot at this desk is then SEEDED into the surface of the same name.
.PP
It is a SEED and not a projection. It applies only where a profile has no
learned slot, and the moment the window is placed the capture loop learns its
real geometry, which wins from then on. So arranging a window by hand at one
desk never overrules what you arranged at another, which is what the
per-profile store exists for.
.PP
NO MAP MEANS NO SEEDING, and that is the opt-out: with no
.BR hwdp ,
no surfaces file for this desk, or a file
.B hwdp
refuses, usher learns only, exactly as before.
.B usher doctor
prints the map and tells those cases apart.
.SH IDENTITY AND PLUGINS
A window is matched to its saved slot by (app_id, key). App-SPECIFIC identity
and respawn live in PLUGINS. Three ship built in, and none keys on what the
window is currently SHOWING: a window that can display many things would
otherwise lose its place whenever you switched:
.TP
.B chrome
keys a window by its SessionID, read from the browser's own session file. That
id is fresh every time Chrome restarts, so a window is followed across the
boundary by the TABS it came back with, joining the previous session file to
the new one. A window Chrome has not recorded (an incognito or guest window
never is) has no identity here and is not remembered at all. Starts the
browser per profile, with
.BR --restore-last-session ,
when the last session had Chrome windows and none is running.
.TP
.B mux
keys a terminal by the COMMAND it runs
.RB ( term:resume ", or " term:latch\ <host>:<session> ),
so switching sessions inside a window does not forfeit its place. Relaunch
replays that command: a
.B mux latch
is reproduced exactly, and anything else was a local mux, for which
.B mux resume
rebuilds the whole recorded set rather than the one session a titlebar happened
to name (a soft dependency: absent \fBmux\fR degrades to a no-op).
.TP
.B kitty
keys any other kitty terminal by its shell's working directory (from /proc) and
respawns it as a shell there.
.PP
A user adds a plugin as a
.I *.py
file in
.I ~/.config/usher/plugins/
defining a top-level
.B PLUGIN
object: see the shipped
.I share/plugins/example.py .
Each plugin claims an app's windows
.RB ( owns )
and may implement
.BR resolve ", " owns_key ", " relaunch_command ", "
.BR relaunch_missing ", and " wind_down ,
each taking a normalized view
.RB ( v["app"] ", " v["title"] ", " v["pid"] ", " v["id"] ).
.I v["id"]
is the compositor's view id, stable for exactly one window's lifetime and
.I None
for a window replayed from the store, so it is what to cache a hard-won
identity against.
.PP
.B resolve
answers in three ways and the third is the point:
.IR ("ready", " key)" ,
.IR ("pending", " why)" ,
.IR ("never", " why)" .
usher neither places nor remembers a window that is not ready, so a plugin
that cannot yet say WHICH window it is must say that rather than guess: a
guessed key is written to the store and then placed against, which is worse
than waiting. The reason is what
.B doctor
prints for an unmatched window, so the plugin that knows supplies it. The
2-tuple keeps plugins free of any usher import.
.PP
.B owns_key
asks the opposite question of a STORED entry, with no live window: could
.B resolve
ever have answered this identity?
.IR True ", " False ", or " None
for "I cannot say", which is the default. usher offers an owned app its
app-id-only key and the key its plugin resolved and nothing else, so a stored
identity a plugin could not have produced can never be looked up again, and
only the 30-day TTL removes it;
.B doctor
reports those. A plugin is the authority on its own key shapes, which is why
this is asked of it rather than tabulated in usher, and
.I None
reads as matchable: naming a working slot as litter invites deleting memory
that was doing its job.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/usher/{exclude,include}
never-place rules and settled-state anchors.
.TP
.I ~/.config/usher/plugins/*.py
user window plugins.
.TP
.I ~/.config/usher/window-source
OPTIONAL hook, asked which account a window belongs to. Run as
.I window-source
.IR PID :
exit 0 with ONE WORD on stdout names that window's source, exit 1 with no
output means the window is the session user's own, and anything else means it
could not tell. usher names no provider and works identically with none,
falling back to comparing the owner of the window's process against the
session user, so the only thing a missing hook costs is the NAME. Asked only
about windows that are already not ours, cached for the life of the process,
and bounded at two seconds.
.IP
A window belonging to another account is never keyed, learned, placed or
relaunched: usher cannot read that account's application state, so it cannot
identify the window, and keying it on a guess corrupts one of this user's own
slots instead.
.TP
.I ~/.config/usher/source-trigger
OPTIONAL hook, asked to make another account act on its OWN windows. Run as
.I source-trigger
.I SOURCE
.BR capture | resume ,
once per source that has windows on this desk. Exit 0 means accepted; anything
else is reported.
.IP
NO CONTENT CROSSES THIS SEAM. Both words come from a closed set usher itself
defines, so there is nothing for the far side to validate and nothing for this
side to inject. An earlier design handed over the app-ids to start, and that
is an escalation rather than a convenience: a recognised terminal app-id
resolves to a terminal on the far side, so the session user could have
arbitrary code run as the other account, which is the one thing the account
boundary exists to prevent.
.IP
THE ARGV IS EXACTLY THREE WORDS AND THE HOOK MUST PASS STDIN THROUGH. usher
writes one
.BI "pid " "<toplevel-identifier> <pid>"
line per window of that source on the child's standard input. That is the one
fact the account cannot get for itself, since
.I ext_foreign_toplevel_list_v1
is the only window protocol a sandboxed client gets and it carries no pid, so
the account cannot map one of its own windows to one of its own processes, and
a terminal's identity lives nowhere else. It travels on stdin rather than in
the argument list BECAUSE the fixed arity is what lets a sudoers grant name
one command with no wildcard. A hook that
.BR exec s
preserves stdin and needs no change; one that drops it costs the account only
the windows it needed a pid to describe.
.IP
usher names no mechanism. Whether the integrator reaches that account by sudo,
by a transient unit, or by dropping a request in a shared directory is its
business, and so is which system account a source name means. Without this
hook another account's windows are placed but never started, and usher says
which accounts it could not reach.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/usher/
the layout store (current snapshot, history, knowledge base).
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/usher/roster.json
which apps THIS account had open, written by
.B usher-mgr _source-capture
and read by
.BR "usher-mgr _source-resume" .
Present only on an account reached through the seam above; the session user's
own layout is the store, not this.
.IP
It also carries, per window, the identity and the COMMAND that brings that one
back, read from this account's own /proc with the relayed pid. An app-id alone
is enough for most apps and not for a terminal, which would otherwise come
back as a bare shell in the home directory having forgotten both its directory
and the session it was showing. No title and no geometry: a title is this
account's content and is redacted everywhere else, and geometry belongs to the
session user.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every knob usher reads, with its default. All are read ONCE at import, so a
running daemon keeps the values it started with: change one and restart it
with
.BR "usher reload" .
.TP
.BI USHER_SKIP_TITLE " ^\e[WORK"
Window titles matching this regex are ignored end to end: never captured,
never placed, never relaunched. This is the work/personal boundary, so widen
it with care and narrow it only if you know nothing sensitive wears the
banner.
.TP
.BI USHER_START_FLOOR " 30"
Seconds after login (or a
.B place
kick) during which every mapped window is placed back. A floor, not a race:
activity EXTENDS it (see
.BR USHER_IDLE_SETTLE ),
so a staggered restore keeps it alive, and
.B usher place
re-arms it by hand when that is not enough. Sized from measurement rather than
caution: restoring a session takes SECONDS, with a twelve-window login
measured back in place within three.
.TP
.BI USHER_IDLE_SETTLE " 15"
Seconds with no new window before placing gives way to settled,
where only
.I include
anchors are moved. This is how big a GAP IN ARRIVALS means the restore is
over, so it tracks how applications spawn rather than how long you want
placing: the widest gap measured during a real restore is 4-5s, and Chrome's
own per-profile stagger is 4s.
.TP
.BI USHER_PLACE_CAP " 180"
Hard cap on the place window, whatever the other two say. It bounds a restore
that keeps producing windows; past it, a window that MAPPED while placing is
still owed one placement when it first becomes identifiable, so a late
identity does not depend on this.
.TP
.BI USHER_DOCK_HOLD " 20"
Seconds that a monitor arriving or leaving suppresses capture for. A dock
coming out makes the compositor move every window on the vanished output, and
learning that would overwrite the slots it just destroyed. Ended early by
hwdp's display-change hook, so this bound only matters on a box without hwdp,
or when the hook is slower than it.
.TP
.BI USHER_CHROME_FLAGS " --restore-last-session --hide-crash-restore-bubble"
Flags added to each Chrome launch, space separated. Empty passes none, which
leaves Chrome to its own startup preference.
.TP
.BI USHER_CHROME_STAGGER " 4"
Seconds between per-profile Chrome launches. Chrome is one process per
user-data-dir, so launching two profiles in the same second loses one of
them.
.TP
.BI USHER_WIND_DOWN_TIMEOUT " 8"
Seconds
.B wind-down
waits for every app it asked to quit. One bounded deadline for all of them,
so no plugin can be the reason a machine will not reboot.
.TP
.BI USHER_EXCLUDE_FILE " ~/.config/usher/exclude"
.TQ
.BI USHER_INCLUDE_FILE " ~/.config/usher/include"
Override the rule files. Losing them is SILENT, since no rules parses fine
and means "place everything".
.TP
.BI USHER_PROFILE " (derived)"
Force the display-profile id instead of deriving it from the connected
monitors. See DISPLAY PROFILES.
.TP
.BI USHER_NO_DEFAULT_RELAUNCH " unset"
Set to 1 to stop usher starting saved windows from the freedesktop registry.
Plugins and
.I exclude
still apply.
.TP
.BI USHER_SUMMONED " unset"
Internal, and never set explicitly.
.B usher cleanly login
exports it into the session it starts, which is how the daemon can tell a
session begun REMOTELY from one begun at the keyboard. It is ONE OF TWO
routes for that signal: the variable travels by exec inheritance, which stops
working if the compositor is ever started as a systemd unit rather than as a
child of the session command, so
.B cleanly login
also leaves a one-shot marker in
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/usher.summoned
that the daemon consumes as it reads. On that signal it locks
the console once, before placing anything, because a summoned session would
otherwise come up logged in and unlocked in front of nobody. Setting it by
hand only makes a console session lock itself at login for no reason.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
did what you asked, and it was fine.
.TP
.B 1
did what you asked; the answer is bad (a contract breach, a failed verify).
.TP
.B 2
could not do it at all: no such verb, no prediction to check against, or no
visible session. Nothing was assessed, which is a different statement from 1.
